---
// Safe to edit. The prose below is the legal/transparency content; rewrite
// any paragraph in place. Bump lastUpdated when meaningful changes ship.
/* ============================================================================
   /privacy | Privacy notice
   ============================================================================
   Short, honest accounting of what the site collects. Contact form,
   Cloudflare analytics beacon, Google Analytics 4 (added 2026-09-04; it
   sets first-party cookies, so the Analytics and Cookies sections name it
   and link the opt-out), server logs. No marketing pixels, no advertising
   trackers. Keep it TRUE to the code and the hosting (CLAUDE.md rule 11).

   Layout: DocumentLayout (overhaul 2.9) gives the H1, "Last updated", the
   sticky "On this page" list at lg and the one prose measure. The `sections`
   array below is the single source for the list and the <h2> ids.
   ============================================================================ */

import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';

const lastUpdated = 'October 18, 2026';

const sections = [
  { id: 'contact-form', label: 'Contact form' },
  { id: 'analytics', label: 'Analytics' },
  { id: 'server-logs', label: 'Server logs' },
  { id: 'cookies', label: 'Cookies' },
  { id: 'your-data', label: 'Your data' },
  { id: 'changes', label: 'Changes' },
];
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="Privacy | Nixon Creative Studio"
  description="What this site collects when you visit or use the contact form, what I do with it, and who handles it, including Cloudflare, Google Analytics and Web3Forms."
  tag="Privacy notice"
  lead="What this site"
  heavy="collects."
  updated={lastUpdated}
  sections={sections}
  headingId="privacy-heading"
>
  <p slot="intro">
    Short version: a contact form, two analytics tools (a cookieless Cloudflare beacon and Google
    Analytics), an embedded Google Map on the contact page, and the things any web server logs. No
    advertising trackers. If you have questions, email <a href={site.emailHref}>{site.email}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contact-form">Contact form</h2>
  <p>
    When you submit the form on the Contact page, the fields you fill in (your name, email,
    organization type, budget range, and project description) are sent through{' '}
    <a href="https://web3forms.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
      Web3Forms
    </a>{' '}
    and delivered to my inbox. Web3Forms acts as the delivery pipe and does not retain submissions
    beyond the brief window needed to deliver them. I keep the email in my inbox the same way I'd
    keep any project inquiry.
  </p>
  <p>
    The form also includes a hidden "honeypot" field that real visitors never fill in. If a bot
    submits the form and trips the honeypot, the message is quietly discarded.
  </p>

  <h2 id="analytics">Analytics</h2>
  <p>
    The site uses two analytics tools, and I use both for the same thing: to see which pages are
    useful and which aren't.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
      Cloudflare Web Analytics
    </a>{' '}
    counts page views and basic performance metrics without cookies, without fingerprinting, and
    without sending data to any third-party advertising network.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a
      href="https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/6004245"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      Google Analytics 4
    </a>{' '}
    records page views, scrolls, and outbound clicks, and sends them to Google. It sets first-party
    cookies to tell returning visits apart, and it truncates IP addresses before storing anything. I
    do not use it for advertising, I have not linked it to any ad account, and I have not turned on
    Google Signals or any demographic reporting. To opt out everywhere, install Google's{' '}
    <a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
      browser add-on
    </a>
    ; most tracker-blocking extensions and browsers also stop it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="server-logs">Server logs</h2>
  <p>
    The hosting platform (Cloudflare) records standard request data: IP address, user agent,
    requested URL, response code, timestamp. These logs are retained for a short window and used for
    operational purposes like blocking abuse and diagnosing errors. They are not used to build
    profiles of visitors.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cookies">Cookies</h2>
  <p>
    Google Analytics sets a small number of first-party cookies (the <code>_ga</code> family) so it
    can tell a returning visit from a new one. They carry a random identifier, not your name or
    email, and the Analytics section above links the opt-out. The contact page also embeds a Google
    Map: when that map loads, Google may set its own cookies and receive your IP address, the same
    as if you visited Google Maps directly. Cloudflare, which hosts the site, may also set a
    short-lived security cookie called <code>cf_clearance</code> when it checks that a visit comes
    from a real browser rather than a bot; it is used only for that check. Nothing else on the site
    sets cookies. If a future change adds more third-party content, this page will say so first.
  </p>

  <h2 id="your-data">Your data</h2>
  <p>
    If you've sent a project inquiry and want me to delete it from my inbox, email{' '}
    <a href={site.emailHref}>{site.email}</a> with the email address you used to send it. I'll
    confirm when it's deleted.
  </p>

  <h2 id="changes">Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If what the site collects changes, I'll update this page and bump the "Last updated" date at the
    top. There's no separate notification list for privacy updates.
  </p>
</DocumentLayout>
